<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Biedermeier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Biedermeier is the name given to the middle-class culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 and the revolutions of 1848. Under censorship and political repression, it turned to private and domestic life, producing simple, comfortable furniture, intimate genre painting, house music and sentimental literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/biedermeier.jpg" alt="Biedermeier" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Spitzweg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carl_Spitzweg_-_Sonntagsspaziergang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
